hdw4s (2.4.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Visitor desktops get more visibly different backgrounds: each one draws
    its colour and lightness from the whole Ubuntu range rather than a few
    fixed steps, and has softly blended lighter and darker sections of its
    own, so two desktops open side by side are easy to tell apart. White icon
    labels on the desktop keep at least the contrast WCAG asks of body text.

  * A template can be moved between machines that cannot reach each other:
    "hdw4s template export <file>" writes it to one archive, and "hdw4s
    template copyfrom" takes that file as well as a host name.

  * Visitor desktop seats are now named _hdw4s_0, _hdw4s_1, ... so that they
    can never be mistaken for a person's account, and the
    HDW4S_EPHEMERAL_PREFIX setting is gone. A machine upgraded from an earlier
    version has to be told its pool size once: "hdw4s check" names the old
    seats, and "hdw4s pool size N" (at the size it had) followed by a reboot
    replaces them. Until then no visitor desktop starts.

 -- Markus Gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0700

hdw4s (2.4.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Visitor desktops can start from a template the administrator prepares:
    "hdw4s template edit" prints a one-time link to a desktop of its own, and
    logging out of it publishes what was changed -- GNOME settings, extensions
    and backgrounds, which folders a home shows, and Chrome's extensions,
    bookmarks and preferences, never its browsing. "template keep" carries a
    program's own settings too, "template reset" goes back to stock, and
    "template copyfrom <host>" takes another machine's template.

  * The pool of visitor desktops is managed as one thing. "hdw4s pool size N"
    grows or shrinks it at once, without a reboot and without ending anybody's
    desktop; "hdw4s pool set" configures every visitor desktop together; and
    "hdw4s list" shows the people's desktops with one line for the pool
    ("--seats" for each seat). Seats no longer use up port numbers.

  * A visitor desktop leaves nothing behind when it ends: its log, crash
    reports and the account records the system kept for it are removed, and it
    can print only through the browser it is shown in.

  * In a visitor desktop, ~/.config and its neighbours are where the settings
    are, the Trash works, the screen can never lock (nobody could unlock it),
    and no update prompts appear. Named desktops get no update prompts either,
    and cannot lock their screen unless HDW4S_SCREEN_LOCK=on.

  * Every desktop starts on WebSockets and can be switched to WebRTC from the
    sidebar, without asking to connect again. If a streaming-server update
    breaks WebRTC, desktops start without it instead of not at all, and
    "hdw4s check" says so.

  * The "your desktop is starting" card no longer covers a desktop that is
    already streaming, and a desktop that briefly drops a page request while
    it starts shows the starting page instead of an error.

 -- Markus Gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 22:30:00 -0700

hdw4s (2.4.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The desktop instrumentation no longer slows the desktop it is measuring.
    One setting in it wrote thirty-five times the usual log volume while a
    desktop was starting, which is the moment somebody is waiting, so a machine
    being watched came up slower than one that was not. It is gone; the part
    that records the desktop's own errors stays.

  * When it is on, it now also records the ANSWERS to the questions it was
    already recording. A reply on that bus carries no interface, so the filter
    that caught every question caught none of the replies -- and the reply is
    the half that says what the desktop was told.

 -- Markus Gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 21:40:00 -0700

hdw4s (2.4.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A desktop you have finished with no longer sits in your list saying it is
    being discarded. The work itself finished in seconds; the page went on
    reporting it indefinitely, because the only thing that cleared the row was
    a visit from the tab you had just closed.

  * Reconnecting to a desktop the browser had stopped no longer flashes the
    "your desktop is starting" card over a picture that is already there. The
    card is meant to stay away when the desktop has already painted, and the
    one moment it could not tell -- a stream the browser had stopped -- was
    exactly the moment it was needed.

  * An administrator can ask a desktop to say what its own startup did, with
    HDW4S_DEBUG_DESKTOP=on. It is off by default and it is loud: it exists for
    chasing a desktop that came up wrong, not for running a machine.

 -- Markus Gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 21:05:00 -0700

hdw4s (2.4.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Closing a tab and asking for a new desktop no longer costs you the old
    slot for a week. A browser that still had the discarded session open would
    retry, reach that slot's door, and start a whole new desktop that nobody
    was looking at -- which then sat in the pool until the idle window expired,
    seven days by default. The router now remembers that it ended a letting and
    refuses to hand the slot back, and a teardown says so for as long as it is
    running rather than for the seventy milliseconds it used to.

  * The "your desktop is starting" card no longer appears for waits that are
    about to end, and no longer gives up on one that is merely slow. It stays
    out of the way for the first moment, fades in only if the desktop really is
    taking a while, and vanishes the instant the picture arrives. If a desktop
    takes much longer than this machine usually does, it now says so and offers
    you another one instead of showing you a black screen.

 -- Markus Gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 16:03:39 -0700

hdw4s (2.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A machine upgraded from an older version could come up, after its next
    reboot, with no desktop accounts and no memory limit on the desktops --
    while its doors were open and reporting healthy. The unit that creates
    both had been left switched off by a package built months earlier, and
    nothing since could switch it back on. Installing this version does.

    It only ever showed after a reboot, because installing repaired the
    machine by hand at the same time as leaving the switch off.

 -- Markus Gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 12:40:00 -0700

hdw4s (2.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Desktops for visitors nobody provisions. A machine can now offer a pool of
    ephemeral desktops on one hostname: anybody who reaches it is given a clean
    desktop of their own, and the browser that started it is the only thing that
    can come back to it. Nothing in such a desktop is kept -- its home and its
    settings live in memory and are destroyed when it ends -- and it ends by
    itself once nobody has connected for a while. Named desktops, one per
    person, are unchanged and can be used on the same machine.

  * A visitor can see the desktops they have, start another, and end one they
    are finished with, from the same page. It works when the machine is
    struggling, which is when somebody needs it: it is plain text with nothing
    to load, and it says how much memory each desktop is using, because the one
    that is misbehaving is usually the one to end.

  * Every ephemeral desktop gets a background of its own, so two of them side by
    side are not the same picture. Two desktops that differed only in the
    address bar were being mixed up.

  * Arriving at a desktop that is already yours no longer costs a click when the
    desktop was created by that very arrival. A desktop that may already be open
    somewhere else still asks first, because connecting takes it away from
    whoever is watching.

  * A desktop cannot reach another desktop's connection on the same machine.

  * The desktop says it is starting, instead of showing a black screen while it
    does.

 -- Markus Gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 12:00:00 -0700

hdw4s (2.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Ephemeral sessions survive a reboot: the slot minter is enabled by both
    installers, and the runtime directories a session cannot start without are
    created by the unit whose job is preconditions rather than by a directive
    that could never have created them.

  * The reaper says what it destroys. An ephemeral slot keeps its home in
    memory, so stopping one discards it; the message claimed otherwise.

  * A gate state carries its own button text, so the card no longer offers to
    take over a desktop it cannot know about.

 -- Markus Gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 16:21:36 -0700

hdw4s (2.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The camera and microphone can be switched on and off from the side menu
    again. They were pinned on, and a pinned setting is one the dashboard draws
    no control for, so there was no way to turn either off from inside a
    session.

